{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md; cat paper_strategy.json; tail -75 paper_strategy.md; command -v apply_patch || true; ls /workspace; rg -n 'textwidth|textheight|column' paper.sty; ls paper_diagnostics -R\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n---\nname: write-paper-standalone\ndescription: Write a grounded LaTeX paper as one coherent standalone scientific study centered on a single defensible thesis rather than the run chronology.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate `paper.tex` and `claims_grounding.json` from grounded artifacts only, with a clean standalone-paper narrative.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper step when the default paper-writing skill keeps drifting into a search diary.\n- Need a standalone scientific paper built around one central method, hypothesis, or comparison story.\n- Need to hide run order and expose only the subset of baselines and ablations that support a coherent grounded manuscript.\n\n# What to do\n1. Read:\n- `results_readme.md` — campaign-level overview of all tasks, variants, and where to find results. Use this to understand the full scope of the campaign beyond the primary task passed via `--task-dir`.\n- `paper_data.json`\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n- `contributions.md` (if provided) — optional base for framing the claimed contributions; not a grounding source\n- `global_memory_summary.md` (if provided)\n- Aggregated CSVs in `/` for fine-grained analysis (install and use pandas) — when available\n- generated figures in `figures/`\n- when `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also follow `presentation_contract` as a visible-language contract\n2. Use `templates/iclr/paper.tex` to keep section order stable. The paper format is **iclr**.\nInspect the selected template and any venue guidance before drafting so the author block, section structure, and framing expectations match the chosen format.\n3. Before drafting, choose one central thesis that is strongly supported by grounded evidence. Do not choose the paper story by recency alone.\n4. Group related implementations into a small number of named method families. Keep only the baseline, proposed method, and ablations needed to support the central thesis.\n5. If the artifacts do not support one coherent paper story, fail clearly instead of narrating the full search process.\n6. Write required sections:\n- abstract\n- introduction\n- related work\n- method\n- results\n- conclusion\n- references\nThe Supplementary Information section is mandatory.\nEmbed it in the same `paper.tex`, after the references, in this order: `\\clearpage`, bibliography, `\\clearpage`, `\\appendix`, and `\\section{Supplementary Information}`.\nReplace the template scaffold with substantive grounded content, including at least one meaningful subsection, table, or figure supported by the available artifacts. Suitable material includes extended methods, reproducibility details, additional ablations, robustness or failure analyses, and extra grounded tables or figures. Never move evidence essential to the central claim out of the main paper.\nRoute `appendix`-ranked posthoc analyses to Supplementary Information by default. Do not promote or drop them merely because of the main-body page budget.\n7. Generate comparative figures with Python + matplotlib by constructing analysis code yourself during the run:\n- inline `python - <<'PY' ... PY`, or\n- ad-hoc scripts in the paper workspace (for reproducibility).\n8. Prefer comparison figures over chronology figures. Use progression-style plots only when they directly support the main scientific claim.\n9. When multiple benchmarks, datasets, or settings are included, organize the visible paper around a shared hypothesis and condition-specific adaptations or limits, not around campaign chronology.\n10. If `paper_strategy.json` includes `figure_specs`, implement those figures first and keep every generated figure within the declared readability budgets.\n11. Use a logically consistent color scheme across all figures in the paper. Reuse the same colors for the same methods, baselines, conditions, or semantic roles whenever they recur, and avoid remapping meanings between figures without a clear reason.\n12. When a `figure_specs[*]` entry sets `preferred_generator` to `generate-concept-figure`, your job is to wire it in, not to render it. Do not invoke `autoillustrate` or the `generate-concept-figure` pipeline. Reference `./figures/<figure_id>.png` in the manuscript, create a lightweight placeholder PNG and `{}` prompt-log stub so the draft compiles, and register `generator: generate-concept-figure` plus `prompt_log_path: figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` in `figure_manifest.json`. The orchestrator replaces those placeholders synchronously after the draft stage. Any reusable placeholder script must skip generation when `.concept_figure_complete` exists so repair rounds cannot overwrite the rendered asset.\n13. Do not satisfy a required overview-style `diagram` figure with a hand-built matplotlib/TikZ schematic unless the concept-figure workflow failed or was impossible; if that fallback happens, record the reason in `./memory.md`, `./global_memory/memory.md`, and the matching `figure_manifest.json` entry.\n14. Write `figure_manifest.json` for every figure referenced in `paper.tex`. Use `../validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json` as the canonical contract. Legacy flat rows and richer nested rows are both acceptable if they validate against that schema.\n15. Record generator provenance in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `generator`, `prompt_log_path`, and `fallback_reason` when a preferred generator was skipped.\nWhen evidence-audit feedback requires changing an orchestrator-owned concept figure, turn the correction into a complete documented fallback: export both `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.pdf`, set `generator` to the actual fallback generator (`matplotlib`, `tikz`, `native_latex`, or `manual`), set `attempted_generator: generate-concept-figure`, retain the original `prompt_log_path`, and set a non-empty `fallback_reason` describing why the orchestrator render was replaced. Use exactly those canonical artifact paths, and do not create or modify `./figures/<figure_id>.png.prov.json`; the orchestrator issues request-bound provenance after accepting the repair. Do not patch only the PNG while leaving `generator: generate-concept-figure`; that falsely claims ownership and allows figure preparation to restore the old render. Do not alter `paper_strategy.json` solely to bypass concept-figure orchestration, and do not create, delete, or modify `.concept_figure_complete`; the orchestrator owns that marker.\n16. Before choosing figure widths, inspect the selected paper template or style files to understand the practical column width, full-width span, and text footprint for the current paper format.\n17. Record final-layout hints in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `placement`, `intended_latex_width_fraction`, and `estimated_min_rendered_font_pt`.\n18. Keep figures readable by default: max 4 total panels, max 3 panels per row, max 6 categories per panel, max 4 series per panel, readable fonts, and abbreviated labels instead of tiny text unless the strategy explicitly allows a denser layout.\n19. Choose each LaTeX figure width from the actual generated figure dimensions, aspect ratio, the inspected template geometry, and intended paper footprint. If a figure becomes unreadable at column width, regenerate/simplify it or place it full-width instead of shrinking the same dense figure further.\n20. If `validation_feedback.json` or `validation_feedback.md` is present, treat it as a repair pass: fix every listed hard error first, rerun validation, and keep iterating until the hard errors are gone or the grounded inputs are insufficient.\n21. Do not create visible section or subsection titles centered on `milestones`, `progression`, `timeline`, `phase`, `stage`, `round`, `Experiment A`, `Experiment B`, `first success`, or similar chronology-shaped labels unless the central claim is explicitly about dynamics.\n22. Do not use phrases like `first valid result`, `subsequent improvements`, `next-best`, `later variants`, or `we then tried` in the visible paper unless that wording is strictly necessary inside a brief protocol description.\n23. Keep visible paper text free of internal pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or `rubric`.\n24. If you need a thresholded rate, name it with one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment rate, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse one label for different task-specific thresholds.\n25. When `paper_data.json.ablation_studies` is non-empty, prefer its variant summaries, replicate statistics, and explicit comparisons over inferring ablations from `all_programs` alone.\n26. Use only numbers present in `paper_data.json`.\n27. Use only citation keys present in `references.bib`.\n28. Use `global_memory_summary.md` only as private qualitative framing; do not use it for quantitative claims or `source_ids`.\n29. If `paper_strategy.md` and `paper_strategy.json` are present, treat them as the framing contract for the final draft; fail clearly if they contradict grounded evidence.\n30. Keep visible paper text free of run-diary wording such as discovery order, iteration order, or agent-loop narration.\n31. Write claim mapping JSON:\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"text\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"SomeKey2024\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n32. Run validator:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis validator also checks that `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` stay in sync, and traces every `@evidence[[...]]` link under `--paper-dir` against the `scientific-evidence/` bundle via the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier. Every link must classify as `resolved` or `anchored`; any `dangling` link is a hard error you must fix before finalization. If paper text carries `@evidence[[...]]` tokens but the bundle is missing, generate it first with `bash ../scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir .`.\n33. During repair passes, also rerun the full `validate-paper` workflow after each fix round so the next round starts from fresh feedback, not stale assumptions.\n34. When venue guidance is present, make the title, abstract, introduction, and conclusion explicitly reader-facing for that venue's scientific questions whenever the grounded evidence supports that framing.\n35. If `./paper_authors.tex` exists, treat it as the canonical author source. Keep `\\input{paper_authors.tex}` inside `\\author{...}` when possible; otherwise copy the exact file contents without edits.\n36. Keep the template's watermark block (`% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---` … `% --- end watermark ---`) in `paper.tex` unchanged; the orchestrator regenerates it before every host compile.\n\n# Outputs\n- `paper.tex`\n- `claims_grounding.json`\n- `figure_manifest.json` when figures are generated or required by strategy\n- Optional extra figures in `figures/` (grounded only, no cross-agent aggregate plots).\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Local Residual Scaling for Compositional Conformal Prediction: Benefits and Limits\",\n  \"central_question\": \"When does training-only local residual scaling improve the coverage–reference-mass tradeoff of split-conformal regions for continuous compositions, and what do homoscedastic and covariate-shift controls reveal about its limits?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"In the evaluated logistic-normal settings, the frozen training-only scale-selection procedure improves measured coverage–reference-mass tradeoffs over global ILR calibration under boundary-concentrated heteroscedasticity, provides no consistent homoscedastic advantage, and performs favorably under the tested shift without establishing shifted-distribution validity or a contribution attributable specifically to quantile stumps.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"This is a controlled empirical study of a positive scale function inside a fixed compositional conformal construction, not a new geometry, universal reliability guarantee, or search-success narrative. Six final seed episodes provide matched global-ILR, raw-simplex and Bonferroni comparisons, two nominal levels, fixed subgroups, reference-mass estimates, controls, and paired aggregate uncertainty. The strong contrast between homoscedastic and boundary-concentrated heteroscedastic conditions supplies a coherent scientific question; shifted cases supply a limited stress test rather than another validity theorem. Direct source inspection identifies a complete ridge/constant/tail-blend selection procedure, but no selected-component diagnostics or matched final ridge-only comparison. Conditional empirical benefits therefore beat both a universal-superiority story and an unsupported stump-specific mechanism story. A latent-count bridge was not implemented and an interaction extension has no usable performance evidence. Publication-worthiness is a framing judgment, not an additional qualification result.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"recentered\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Edit mode is not evidenced: no resolved edit-context file, previous_paper directory, aggregated directory, or extra_tasks directory is present. No prior manuscript is claimed to have been revised. The enum 'recentered' describes centering the broad source-task/proposal context on the actually evaluated local-scale procedure. Compared candidate framings were: preserve broad compositional reliability or latent-target motivation; broaden to a shared count-noise/interaction/scale family; recenter on matched residual-scale evidence. The first two exceed the implemented and evaluated scope; the third is selected.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"No prior manuscript or separate staged new/old implementation package was supplied; edit placeholders remain unresolved. Task and evaluated implementation files, rather than a prior title or figure plan, determine the story.\",\n    \"The final_evaluation object was read independently of downstream_study, which is absent. The declared qualification sources report completed, qualified, and true; this run has no failed final qualification to disclose. Candidate self-diagnostics are explicitly unverified, and unspecified gate diagnostics remain unknown.\",\n    \"Direct inspection of results/ideas/02_cross_fitted_tail_adaptive_residual_scaling/program_1/src/program.py and METHOD.md establishes the full training-only scale-selection family. The final result manifest and program-tree hash identify the frozen evaluated selection; no exact-byte hash match between the workspace source and frozen tree was independently recomputed, so source-to-final linkage is documented rather than newly cryptographically verified.\",\n    \"task/evaluate_final.py and task/resources/final/manifest.json establish H/B/S logistic-normal conditions in dimensions 3 and 10, distinct center/scale/calibration/test splits, shared centers, fixed groups and reference probes. These change the research scope from generic classifier probabilities to continuous simplex-valued responses.\",\n    \"The original quadratic-feature ridge scale is available as code and search context, but not as a matched repeated-final comparator. Do not substitute the global-ILR baseline for it or compare its search score to final scores. No study.* sources exist here, so do not report selection deltas or scored/unscored attempt counts as study-grounded findings.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Training-only local scale selection in ILR space\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The frozen evaluated procedure selects among a quadratic-feature ridge log-scale, a constant, and four ridge/tail log-space blends using three training folds, fitting-only alignment, and a one-standard-error heuristic. Describe and evaluate the entire procedure, not a proven quantile-stump winner.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Global ILR split conformal\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Primary matched comparator: same training-only ridge center, ILR Euclidean residual geometry, calibration split and corrected rank, without covariate-dependent scaling. It isolates scale adaptivity more cleanly than cross-geometry comparisons and is not the original ridge-scale implementation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Raw-simplex L-infinity split conformal\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Grounded alternative representation and region shape, evaluated on the same target compositions, splits, centers and reference probes. It contextualizes the ILR comparison without claiming geometry is independently ablated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Coordinatewise Bonferroni regions\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Grounded simultaneous-coordinate comparator using corrected per-coordinate calibration at alpha divided by dimension; expose its coverage and reference-mass tradeoffs rather than reducing comparison to one aggregate score.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Constant-scale and permuted-residual controls\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Training controls examine scale invariance and the dependence on covariate–residual alignment. Constant scale numerically reproduces global ILR; do not plot it as another independent baseline. Permuted residuals support an alignment interpretation but do not isolate stumps, blend weights, or the selection rule.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Calibration and degenerate-region sanity comparators\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Uncalibrated residual quantiles, the full simplex and a singleton center are available for supplementary checks of coverage–mass interpretation. They are not competing learned methods and need not dominate the main narrative.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Main comparison: four scientifically meaningful methods, grouped by homoscedastic (H), boundary-concentrated heteroscedastic (B), and shifted heteroscedastic (S) conditions, with dimension 3 versus 10 treated as adaptations under one thesis rather than independent experiments.\",\n    \"Show coverage at nominal 0.95 and 0.90 separately, not only absolute errors. Show reference mass separately from coverage so that an apparently small region cannot hide undercoverage. Efficiency is probability mass under the fixed mixed probe distribution, not geometric volume, expected cardinality, or a distribution-free quantity.\",\n    \"Keep fixed-subgroup evidence in the main paper: a compact six-condition table compares the local procedure, global ILR and permuted-residual control using mean absolute subgroup coverage error over both groups and both levels. Supporting tables retain the signed per-group, per-level coverage and per-seed Wilson intervals; empirical group diagnostics are not conditional guarantees.\",\n    \"Homoscedastic cases show little benefit and small aggregate-utility disadvantages relative to global ILR; do not omit them. Boundary heteroscedastic cases support the strongest joint subgroup-error/reference-mass comparison. Boundary concentration and heteroscedasticity change together, so neither factor is independently causally identified.\",\n    \"Shifted cases have favorable observed coverage relative to the matched global comparator, but exchangeability is not supplied by the unweighted algorithm. In shifted dimension 3 the local method has larger reference mass than global ILR, so do not claim uniformly smaller regions or a free efficiency gain.\",\n    \"An aggregate main-text table may report mean utility 0.8772268474776957 with its stored 95% seed-bootstrap interval [0.8766236679621646, 0.8781275287778212], mean marginal absolute coverage error 0.011729600694444453, mean fixed-group absolute coverage error 0.015105333854689552, and reference mass 0.4508402082655165, with sensible rounding. Sources: final.metric.* entries with matching names. Define utility from evaluator code as 1 - [0.375 marginal error + 0.375 group error + 0.25 reference mass]; it is a chosen evaluation weighting, not universal reliability.\",\n    \"The aggregate local-minus-global utility difference is 0.04762325792650014 (final.metric.global_ilr_score_gap); its stored paired six-seed bootstrap interval is [0.043901240346666696, 0.050616967867983816] (final.evidence.episodes). Interpret it as a fixed-six-case aggregate, not evidence of per-case superiority or proof that stumps improve the original ridge-scale method.\",\n    \"Final uncertainty uses six seeds [1009, 2003, 3001, 4001, 5003, 6007] and stored 2000-resample paired seed-bootstrap intervals. Repeated copies of the same aggregate interval inside episodes are one estimate, not six independent estimates. Coverage Wilson intervals describe within-seed test hits; do not average interval endpoints or use a mass Wilson interval as an independent-query population interval.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Figure 1, early conceptual overview: interior-simplex response -> ILR center and training-only positive scale -> held-out normalized-score calibration -> separate coverage, subgroup and reference-mass evaluation; mark the shifted condition outside the exchangeability guarantee.\",\n    \"Figure 2, main reliability comparison: four panels in a 2-by-2 grid for dimension and nominal level, comparing the four main methods across H/B/S with all six seed points and an arithmetic-mean marker. Nominal coverage is a horizontal line, not an attainment-rate label.\",\n    \"Figure 3, companion efficiency comparison: two dimension panels comparing reference mass across H/B/S and the same four methods, explicitly averaged over the two nominal levels within each seed. Read alongside Figure 2 and the main subgroup table, never as a standalone superiority ranking.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"conceptual_overview\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Make the target, training/calibration separation, learned scale, and limited meaning of the evidence legible before quantitative results.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"scientific_object_and_information_split\",\n      \"metric\": \"not_applicable_conceptual_only\",\n      \"split_by\": \"training_calibration_and_evaluation_roles\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"The inspected ICLR paper.sty gives one 5.5-inch text column and a 9-inch text height; full width is also column width here. Target a 5.5-by-1.9-inch overview on page 1 or 2, about 0.25 page including caption. One left-to-right schematic with at most six short labeled blocks and a shift caveat is readable at 9-point text; avoid mini-panels, data-shaped decorative regions and invented observations.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"coverage_by_condition\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Expose condition-dependent empirical reliability, both nominal levels, and the absence of a uniform advantage.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"faceted_seed_dot_plot\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"H_B_S_condition\",\n      \"metric\": \"empirical_joint_composition_coverage\",\n      \"split_by\": \"dimension_3_or_10_and_nominal_coverage_0.95_or_0.90\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Use the full 5.5-inch single-column width, approximately 4.2 inches high, about 0.55 page including caption. Four panels in two rows leave each plotting area roughly 2.5 inches wide; restrict to three condition labels, four method colors, six small offset seed dots per method and one shared legend. Labels at least 9 points, ticks at least 8 points; use the same coverage axis across panels and avoid connecting H/B/S as a trajectory. Show seed variability rather than inventing condition-specific bootstrap intervals.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 4,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 3,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"reference_mass_by_condition\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Compare the specific measured size proxy without hiding coverage failures or the shifted dimension-3 mass increase.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"faceted_seed_dot_plot\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"H_B_S_condition\",\n      \"metric\": \"reference_mass_averaged_over_nominal_levels_within_seed\",\n      \"split_by\": \"dimension_3_or_10\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Target 5.5 by 2.5 inches across two side-by-side panels, about 0.35 page including caption. Three categories and four shared method series fit without shrinking labels; use seed dots, mean markers, a shared legend and a common mass axis. State the alpha averaging and mixed reference measure explicitly. Do not combine with the four-panel coverage figure into an unreadable six-panel figure.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 3,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 4\n    }\n  ],\n  \"supplementary_plan\": {\n    \"purpose\": \"Provide reconstructable methods, complete observed uncertainty and diagnostics, and transparency about missing comparisons after references, while leaving every result and limitation essential to the central thesis in the main paper.\",\n    \"planned_subsections\": [\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Complete Conditional Scale-Selection Specification\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Use the inspected selected program and METHOD.md for the quadratic ridge features, tail target 0.9, 15 candidate threshold quantiles, minimum leaf size, learning rate 0.1, maximum 80 stump updates, three deterministic folds, constant and four log blends, fitting-only alignment, simplicity ordering, all-data refit, scale floors and evaluator clipping. Explain that the one-standard-error rule is a heuristic, not a significance test. Include pseudocode derived from source, not selected-component frequencies that were never recorded. Final evidence binds the evaluated method; source linkage is documentary unless independently verified later.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Generator, Splits, Geometry and Reference Measure\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Document task/evaluate_final.py and task/resources/final/manifest.json: six-dimensional Gaussian covariates; H/B/S generator equations; dimension 3/10; center-fit, scale-fit, calibration and test sizes 1024/1024/512/2048; the test first-covariate shift 1.25; shared ridge center; exact ILR basis; strictly positive responses; 128 selected test covariates and 1024 probes per query from a half Gaussian-in-ILR/half uniform-simplex mixture. Explain query clustering, data-dependent reference covariance, and why mass is not geometric volume. No exact-zero, finite-count or real-data experiment is implied.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Full Seed-Level Coverage and Efficiency Tables\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Tabulate all final cases and seeds, both alphas, both fixed sign-of-first-covariate groups, all four main methods, and available within-seed Wilson intervals. Retain signed coverage deviations as well as absolute errors and reference mass; do not infer general conditional coverage. Show stored aggregate paired intervals once and describe the fixed-case, seed-resampling unit. Tables can be generated solely from the final.evidence.episodes pointer.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n          \"final.metric.marginal_coverage_error\",\n          \"final.metric.group_coverage_error\",\n          \"final.metric.reference_mass\",\n          \"final.metric.global_ilr_score_gap\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Alignment, Calibration and Degenerate-Region Checks\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Report constant/global numerical agreement, permuted-training-residual results, uncalibrated residual-quantile baseline, full-simplex and singleton-center coverage/mass, using their stored final records. State what each check tests and cannot establish. Do not invent a stump-only, ridge-only, no-selection, interaction-depth, sample-size, dimension-sweep or weighted-shift ablation.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n          \"final.metric.constant_control_score\",\n          \"final.metric.permuted_control_score\",\n          \"final.metric.control_pass_fraction\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Conditional Rank Argument and Its Boundaries\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Give the standard split-conformal rank argument conditional on the center-fit and scale-fit data: a fixed positive scale preserves exchangeable calibration/test scores; k=ceil((m+1)(1-alpha)) with infinity for k>m gives marginal coverage under the stated assumptions. Cover ties and common clipping. Do not label it a new theorem, a group-coverage guarantee, or shifted-distribution validity; novelty and supporting citations need independent literature verification.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Reproducibility and Evaluation Scope\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Document the available NumPy 2.1.3 requirement, evaluator Python 3.12 statement, six final seeds, synthetic-data generation and no external-data download, artifact hashes, available commands and per-seed stored elapsed durations with their limited meaning. Distinguish inspected code, recorded executions and commands verified later. Transparently record that the latent-count bridge was not implemented and the interaction attempt has no usable measured performance; root cause is unknown. Search counts and deltas are omitted because study.* sources are absent. Final qualification is completed/qualified, not proof of generalization. Provide anonymous relative reproduction paths and a table/figure-to-generator mapping when those scripts exist.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n          \"final.qualification.status\",\n          \"final.qualification.outcome\",\n          \"final.qualification.correct\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"LLM Usage\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Disclose the evidenced roles of LLMs in the current framing interaction and available proposal, source-editing and reference-query process artifacts, then add the precise writing role when it occurs. This is process provenance, not an episode-derived scientific result, so no paper_data result source ID is assigned. Inspect actual logs before attributing additional roles or model versions; do not assert unrecorded manual verification or completed experiments. Record prohibited-literature encounters only if documented; this strategy made no network requests.\",\n        \"source_ids\": []\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"forbidden_headings\": [\n    \"Search Progression\",\n    \"Campaign Phases\",\n    \"Milestones\",\n    \"First Valid Result\",\n    \"Iteration History\",\n    \"Experiment A\",\n    \"Experiment B\",\n    \"Winner Selection\",\n    \"From Failure to Success\",\n    \"Latent-Count Bridge Results\",\n    \"Quantile-Stump Ablation\"\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_phrases\": [\n    \"we then tried\",\n    \"subsequent improvements\",\n    \"first valid result\",\n    \"next-best\",\n    \"later variants\",\n    \"search winner\",\n    \"correctness rate\",\n    \"correct and eligible\",\n    \"eligible\",\n    \"combined_score\",\n    \"rubric\",\n    \"worker_01\",\n    \"worker_02\",\n    \"state of the art\",\n    \"universally reliable\",\n    \"uniformly smaller regions\",\n    \"guaranteed coverage under covariate shift\",\n    \"distribution-free conditional validity\",\n    \"quantile stumps caused the gain\",\n    \"the latent bridge restored coverage\",\n    \"unscored attempts were measured losses\"\n  ],\n  \"notes_for_writer\": [\n    \"Draft no paper during strategy generation. This plan is for write-paper-standalone. Later writing, review, audit and revision must independently re-read final.qualification.*; retain any failed qualification and its limitations if the authoritative artifacts change. Here completed/qualified/true is explicit; unspecified diagnostics remain unknown, and no statistical-versus-adaptivity cause should be invented.\",\n    \"Qualification source IDs are final.qualification.status, final.qualification.outcome and final.qualification.correct. Scientific result records are final.evidence.episodes and final.metric.*. The episode grounding entry is a pointer, not an embedded value; follow its json_pointer to the actual episodes. Source-inspected equations and implementation facts additionally require the named local files; do not imply those details were candidate diagnostics verified by the evaluator.\",\n    \"Use ICLR 2026 per VENUE.md, not a newly chosen conference. Inspected .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex and paper.sty: a single 5.5-inch column, 9-inch text height, normal float width equal to text width. All required figures use preferred_span=full_width meaning that sole text column; no fictitious two-column 0.48-width placement. Keep body between 8 and 9 pages; target 8.5 substantive pages and at least 75% occupancy if ending at 8 pages.\",\n    \"Body allocation including associated floats: abstract/introduction with early overview 1.25 pages; focused related work 0.65; target, geometry, scale procedure and short rank justification 1.85; experimental design, metrics, controls and uncertainty 1.15; condition-structured results with coverage/mass figures and two concise tables 2.40; discussion and limitations 0.95; conclusion 0.25. Total 8.50 pages. Use extra space for the exact reference-measure definition, subgroup contrasts and mechanism caveats, never chronology, repeated tables or generic filler. Keep the main-text limitations and negative H results even if page pressure requires moving detailed pseudocode after references.\",\n    \"Suggested main headings: Introduction; Related Work; Compositional Regions and Training-Only Scale Learning; Evaluation Design; Coverage and Efficiency Across Conditions; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. Within results use Homoscedastic Controls, Boundary-Concentrated Heteroscedasticity, and Covariate-Shift Stress Test. These are three conditions of one study, not three discovery stages.\",\n    \"Main Table 1: four-method aggregate utility and decomposed errors/mass, with stored local-minus-comparator paired intervals explicitly aggregate over the fixed six cases. Main Table 2: six-condition local/global/permuted subgroup-error comparison. Do not manufacture cell-specific confidence intervals from the overall paired interval; distinguish descriptive means from stored inferential summaries.\",\n    \"Required quantitative figures must derive every seed point from final.evidence.episodes, export PDF and PNG from the same source with PNG at 300 DPI, and use the PDF in LaTeX whenever available. All full-width raster-only assets must be regenerated at at least 2400 pixels wide, never upscaled; any later column-width-only raster must be at least 1200 pixels wide. At most one overview uses generate-concept-figure; do not create that image in this strategy stage. Preserve editable concept source and provenance later, and record any necessary generator fallback rather than silently changing ownership.\",\n    \"The shared reference distribution is a training-dependent mixture, not invariant simplex volume. Probe-hit Wilson intervals are evaluator-provided binomial-style summaries over clustered query/probe hits; they do not quantify all query-sampling uncertainty. Six seeds and a fixed condition grid limit bootstrap precision and external validity. Do not pool all test examples across seeds as if one independently fitted model generated them.\",\n    \"Do not equate composition-region coverage with class-label prediction-set coverage, calibration of class probabilities, latent multinomial-probability recovery, or true conditional-probability identification. Outputs here are strictly positive continuous logistic-normal responses, not softmax predictions from an evaluated classifier, exact-zero compositions, CIFAR data or finite-count observations.\",\n    \"No recorded component-selection frequency, matched repeated ridge-scale comparison, interaction ablation, sensitivity study or real-data test exists. Neither control success nor a favorable aggregate utility attributes the effect to quantile stumps. The original ridge scale remains a documented component and an untested matched-final comparator, not a demonstrated inferior method.\",\n    \"References and provenance exist, but retrieval metadata alone does not establish relevance or that full text was read. Lei2016DistributionFreePI has a directly relevant title match and is in the allowed starter lineage; verify actual consulted content before citation. Do not fill Related Work with unrelated automatically retrieved entries. Verify suitable ILR, quantile and conditional-coverage context through permitted sources before claiming novelty. Do not identify, search for, open or use the prohibited related paper or citing/discussing pages; stop and record any accidental encounter. This strategy made no network requests.\",\n    \"For double-blind review remove author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding and identifying links; do not copy the template's explicit author or final-copy setting. Preserve any required automatically managed watermark block. Include separate LLM Usage after references and any actually required venue checklist; do not invent a checklist requirement from another conference or claim this local style was independently certified as the latest official release.\",\n    \"Supplementary source_ids are registered result anchors for the numerical evidence; implementation, resource and process statements additionally name their actual documentary sources and are not recast as measured findings. LLM Usage deliberately has an empty source_ids list because it is grounded in process artifacts and this interaction, not in a paper_data scientific-result entry. No definitive posthoc analysis ranking is assigned; route the separately authoritative appendix-ranked analyses to Supplementary Information without revising this plan retroactively.\",\n    \"Keep qualitative memory private. Do not use global_memory_summary.md, idea names, or old incomplete status prose to override completed final results. Internal names inside this strategy are private-only grounding instructions, never recommended visible manuscript labels. No final-paper validation, paper.tex, or claims_grounding.json is produced in this stage.\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n\n- **Required; generator:** `generate-concept-figure`; the only overview assigned to that workflow.\n- **Purpose:** interior-simplex response → ILR center and training-only positive scale → held-out normalized-score calibration → separate coverage, subgroup and reference-mass evaluation. A short callout places shift outside the exchangeability guarantee.\n- **Footprint:** page 1 or 2, approximately 5.5 × 1.9 inches, about 0.25 page with caption.\n- **Budget:** one panel, one panel per row, at most six labeled blocks, no more than four visual series; 9-point labels or larger.\n- **Safety:** conceptual only, no invented observed points, numeric results or data-shaped example regions. A workflow schematic explains the method, not discovery order.\n\n### Figure 2 — Coverage Across Conditions\n\n- **Required; generator:** `matplotlib`; faceted seed-dot plot.\n- **Panels:** 2 × 2, dimension by nominal level. Each panel has H/B/S and four methods: local scaling, global ILR, raw L-infinity and Bonferroni.\n- **Footprint:** 5.5 × approximately 4.2 inches, about 0.55 page including caption.\n- **Budget:** four total panels, two per row, three categories per panel, four series. Six small offset seed dots per method and an arithmetic-mean marker; one shared legend.\n- **Readability:** common coverage axis, horizontal nominal target, label text at least 9 points and ticks at least 8 points. No lines implying a trajectory through H/B/S. Show existing seed variability without fabricated condition-specific intervals.\n\n### Figure 3 — Reference Mass Across Conditions\n\n- **Required; generator:** `matplotlib`; two dimension panels with the same four methods and three condition categories.\n- **Metric:** within-seed mean reference mass over the two nominal levels. Caption names that averaging and the mixed reference measure.\n- **Footprint:** 5.5 × approximately 2.5 inches, about 0.35 page with caption.\n- **Budget:** two panels in one row, three categories per panel, four series; seed dots and mean markers, shared scale and legend, same typography as Figure 2.\n- **Readability:** do not merge Figures 2 and 3 into a dense six-panel figure. Pair this size view with the coverage plot and main subgroup table.\n\n**Export contract:** quantitative plotting source must export both PDF and PNG, with PNG at 300 DPI, and LaTeX must reference the PDF when present. Raster-only full-width assets require regeneration at least 2400 pixels wide; any later narrower column-width-only raster requires at least 1200 pixels. Never upscale an existing image. Preserve editable concept source and generator provenance; document an actual fallback if needed rather than silently replacing the overview. No figure is generated at this stage.\n\n## Main-Paper Structure and Page Budget\n\nTarget **8.5 substantive body pages**, satisfying the venue's 8–9-page rule; if the body ends at 8 pages, its last page must be at least 75% occupied. Allocations include associated floats:\n\n| Component | Pages | Scientific value |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| Abstract and Introduction, including early overview | 1.25 | Target and question, bounded contributions, glance-value explanation |\n| Related Work | 0.65 | Consulted foundations, ILR context and distinction from neighboring quantile methods |\n| Compositional Regions and Training-Only Scale Learning | 1.85 | Complete procedure, center/scale/calibration separation, short conditional rank argument |\n| Evaluation Design | 1.15 | Generator, comparators, groups, reference measure and uncertainty |\n| Coverage and Efficiency Across Conditions | 2.40 | Two quantitative figures, aggregate table and subgroup table; negative controls remain visible |\n| Discussion and Limitations | 0.95 | Confounded condition changes, missing attribution, shift and external-validity limits |\n| Conclusion | 0.25 | Direct answer within the tested scope |\n| **Total** | **8.50** | No generic filler or chronology |\n\nSubheadings in results should identify **Homoscedastic Controls**, **Boundary-Concentrated Heteroscedasticity**, and **Covariate-Shift Stress Test**. If space remains, improve the exact reference-measure explanation, scale-selection description and subgroup contrasts. If space is tight, move extended pseudocode after references—not the failed/negative controls or central limitations.\n\nTable 1 compares the four main methods' aggregate utility, errors and mass, with stored paired local-minus-comparator intervals explicitly labeled as aggregate. Table 2 compares local/global/permuted subgroup errors across the six conditions. Do not turn the original ridge-scale code into a nonexistent final-results column.\n\n## Supplementary Information After References\n\nPurpose: substantive reconstructability and complete observed diagnostics, not a hiding place for evidence essential to the thesis. No speculative future ablation or posthoc ranking is assigned. Separately supplied authoritative appendix-ranked analyses should also go here without retroactively altering this strategy.\n\n1. **Complete Conditional Scale-Selection Specification.** Source-derived pseudocode and all actual settings: tail quantile 0.9, 15 threshold quantiles, minimum leaf size, learning rate 0.1, maximum 80 updates, three deterministic folds, four blend weights, fitting-only alignment, simplicity order, refit and numerical clipping. The one-standard-error rule is a heuristic, not a test. Do not invent component-selection frequencies. Anchor: `final.evidence.episodes`; documentary sources are the selected implementation and `METHOD.md`, not unverified candidate diagnostics.\n2. **Generator, Splits, Geometry and Reference Measure.** Full equations from `task/evaluate_final.py`, six-dimensional covariates, shift 1.25, dimensions, split sizes, ridge center, ILR basis, strict positivity, probe mixture and query-clustering limits. Anchor: `final.evidence.episodes`; documentary source: final resource manifest and evaluator.\n3. **Full Seed-Level Coverage and Efficiency Tables.** All cases/seeds/levels/groups for the four main methods, signed coverage deviations, reference mass and recorded Wilson intervals. Describe aggregate bootstrap intervals once. Anchors: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.marginal_coverage_error`, `final.metric.group_coverage_error`, `final.metric.reference_mass`, `final.metric.global_ilr_score_gap`.\n4. **Alignment, Calibration and Degenerate-Region Checks.** Constant/global agreement, permuted residuals, uncalibrated quantiles, full-simplex and singleton-center checks; explain exactly what each tests. Anchors: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.constant_control_score`, `final.metric.permuted_control_score`, `final.metric.control_pass_fraction`.\n5. **Conditional Rank Argument and Its Boundaries.** Standard rank proof conditional on center/scale training, corrected rank with infinity when necessary, ties and common scale clipping. Not a new theorem, subgroup guarantee or shift theorem. Anchor: `final.evidence.episodes`; construction details come from the evaluator and selected method source.\n6. **Reproducibility and Evaluation Scope.** Recorded Python 3.12/NumPy 2.1.3 statements, final seeds, synthetic generation, anonymous relative commands, hashes and figure/table-generator mapping when implemented. Stored per-seed elapsed durations are not new method-only runtime measurements. Disclose unimplemented count-noise work, the unscored interaction extension, unknown failure cause, absent matched final ridge-scale evidence, and omitted study-style search counts/deltas. Anchors: `final.evidence.episodes` and `final.qualification.*`; process facts require their actual local records, not inference from an episode pointer.\n7. **LLM Usage.** Required separate section. Disclose assistance evidenced by the current framing interaction and available proposal, source-editing and reference-query process artifacts; add the precise writing role when it occurs. Inspect actual logs before attributing further roles or model versions. Do not claim unrecorded manual checks or call unexecuted experiments completed. Its JSON `source_ids` list is intentionally empty: process provenance is not grounded in a scientific-result episode. Record prohibited-literature encounters only if documented. This strategy made no network requests.\n\n## Literature, Anonymity and Writing Safeguards\n\n`references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` exist. Retrieval metadata is not evidence that a paper was read or even relevant. `Lei2016DistributionFreePI` is a directly relevant metadata match in the allowed starter lineage; actual consulted content still requires verification before citation. Do not pad related work with irrelevant retrieval results. Verify appropriate ILR, quantile-learning and conditional-coverage sources through permitted material before making novelty claims. Do not search for or identify the prohibited related paper, expand citation networks into it, or read citing/discussing pages. Stop and document an actual accidental encounter.\n\nUse ICLR 2026 as required by `VENUE.md`. Do not claim this local style was independently certified as the latest official release. The inspected scaffold contains a named author and final-copy switch: those must not be copied into the anonymous review manuscript. Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding and identifying links. Preserve automatically managed watermark blocks. Include `LLM Usage` and any actually required ICLR checklist, not an invented checklist borrowed from another venue.\n\n### Forbidden Headings\n\nSearch Progression; Campaign Phases; Milestones; First Valid Result; Iteration History; Experiment A; Experiment B; Winner Selection; From Failure to Success; Latent-Count Bridge Results; Quantile-Stump Ablation.\n\n### Forbidden Phrases\n\n“we then tried”; “subsequent improvements”; “first valid result”; “next-best”; “later variants”; “search winner”; “correctness rate”; “correct and eligible”; “eligible”; “combined_score”; “rubric”; “worker_01”; “worker_02”; “state of the art”; “universally reliable”; “uniformly smaller regions”; “guaranteed coverage under covariate shift”; “distribution-free conditional validity”; “quantile stumps caused the gain”; “the latent bridge restored coverage”; “unscored attempts were measured losses”.\n\nInternal field names and pipeline labels in this memo are **private-only grounding context**, never recommended visible manuscript terminology. Memory is private qualitative context, not numerical grounding. Old implementation-stage statements of incomplete execution must not override completed final evaluation evidence.\n\n## Stage Boundary\n\nThis strategy writes only the framing memo and JSON, with append-only memory updates. It does not draft `paper.tex`, write `claims_grounding.json`, generate figures, run experiments, or run final-paper validation. Strategy-schema, grounding-ID and consistency checks are appropriate here. The final writer must preserve the scope, qualification facts, negative controls and limitations throughout drafting and audit.\nfinal_evaluation\ninvestigation\nmetrics\npaper\npipeline_events.jsonl\ntask\ntask_drafting\n48:\\textheight 9.0 true in       % Height of text (including footnotes & figures)\n49:\\textwidth 5.5 true in        % Width of text line.\n83:\\def\\@maketitle{\\vbox{\\hsize\\textwidth\n170:% Less leading in most fonts (due to the narrow columns)\n215:\\textheight=#5\\vbadness=10000\\vfuzz=120ex\\overfullrule=0pt%\n216:\\global\\setbox\\iclrrulerbox=\\vbox to \\textheight{%\n217:{\\parskip=0pt\\hfuzz=150em\\cv@boxheight=\\textheight\n229:\\def\\iclrruler#1{\\makevruler[12pt][#1][1][3][0.993\\textheight]\\usebox{\\iclrrulerbox}}\n232:\\iclrruleroffset=\\textheight\n245:% \\colorbox[rgb]{0.7,0.7,0.7}{\\small \\parbox[b][\\textheight]{0.1cm}{}}}\npaper_diagnostics:\nreference_query_plan\n\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan:\nlast_round.json\nround_0000\n\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000:\nattempt_001\n\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001:\ndiagnostic_state.json\nreference_query_plan.json\nreference_query_plan.md\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
